#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d86c6bd2-e528-55ed-bbfc-9f5ecbec024e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5924" :question/problem "Graph the function $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/9d/9da0db2e6c97e507e82b7ca801e46802fabf91ad31ff53ad3cc9ae580151370a.png)\n\n- Translate it by one unit to the right to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.$ \n\n![](images/8c/8ce8c53f202f04b3b34a247b797f65f7616e8772afa1751de126ddd401417bd7.png)\n\nNotice that the vertical asymptote is now $x=1.$\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9b708a20d9c6c12f0016279e8d7a3ea55597e7645f0a9518f1c32e8a8406e47.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit down to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{2}{x-1}-1.$ \n\n![](images/e1/e100b552adb71cae1295492a58b0e60525813adb87d9815ba64a151a856f0c46.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote is now $y=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308f3685-0a90-56f3-9653-a83cec4286d2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ac8b39e3-a9d0-56fd-8c0b-4d748bedf7d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5926" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb46d056a8d8010eca509543c231c875f4980a21f1af17505fa98c8c64106207.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y = \\dfrac{a}{x-b}+k.$ What is the value of $a+b+k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=-1,$ while the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$ This means that $k=-1$ and $b=2,$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{a}{x-2}+(-1) = \\dfrac{a}{x-2}-1.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x-2}-1 \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{a}{3-2}-1 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+k = 3+2+(-1) = 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068655 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea2f1348-55bf-5313-8d5e-a5a097231e97" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a76e0252-0574-520e-bb5f-43c8faf6a564" :question/math-academy-id "e-8658" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{7}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = \\dfrac{7}{2x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e80865b0f32a5473da27b39537034bcdabee0a6c24032f41413e20518642d3f4.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{7}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\dfrac{7}{2x}.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7b1fe93de798c0aa91f60f66a2507869dbe837b843b7233972e1a78ee22576a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068656 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e11164-1929-594d-9f9e-460f6469421d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Negative Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a18ff1e7-7f87-5cdd-9a25-91a2e9c1dd5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8761" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y = -\\dfrac{1}{5x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y = -\\dfrac{1}{5x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 1 5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{5x}.$\n\n![](images/30/30ace3126ec127b9949ebf599d615020b9770458825d22460e9e7e0b3d5efec3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d489bd3-6352-54ec-b54e-a432c6b2fa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6798ba049ab7040eec51d2da525d3383816578423e11384ced1f3665a2e50fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244851} {:answer/id #uuid "42b5629a-dc31-5bf8-b5b8-068b5beb09e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/7273872baaf62048b257d411f344b91dfe0dc399356cdbfec53237e15dd40105.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244897} {:answer/id #uuid "47117017-97d5-5447-9f53-7f1fc6e47fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/ea9bd36b532b46d3edcc99497b77e75288499a4b02239f5d2416cff6a4259478.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244947} {:answer/id #uuid "96323b66-7c91-53d1-b8ae-202cb322dae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7c180f664a739b00a68e88387f620322eaf0104bcdb45d4f54efe463048bc39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245637} {:answer/id #uuid "ad17b8d4-96c0-5360-9dc6-8abb71004ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/503f584dd13e23429159f6ec03bdc40d411a539fd19d28a72b1ba10446fb1926.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d489bd3-6352-54ec-b54e-a432c6b2fa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6798ba049ab7040eec51d2da525d3383816578423e11384ced1f3665a2e50fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244851} :answer-field/id #uuid "4861429f-0147-5467-8215-956a4587922f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "097f9b36-1f3f-531e-bec1-feea7bb61a3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113372" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{5}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea9bd36b532b46d3edcc99497b77e75288499a4b02239f5d2416cff6a4259478.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units up to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+3.$\n\n![](images/e6/e6798ba049ab7040eec51d2da525d3383816578423e11384ced1f3665a2e50fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ac99bf6-327a-5511-b7a4-734f9c76a5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b9683bcc1b559505141d45b9007ef45011adee98b33730ffad1876a462d9abc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395372} {:answer/id #uuid "d352e845-d658-517b-b4e5-85b07a890741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/804eac3df07358686b859f20630acc5344f82ff0c71dc80e6320ab2f7b2313f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395373} {:answer/id #uuid "b31d701f-35d2-53fb-9bb7-6e53d677c6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a2/a2af9de8793d7f2e527721b3874ac6d49d25d0a1e496d3cd62ecf2710b4ab932.pn